**Sec. 70401**
This section enhances the employer-provided child care credit by increasing the percentage of qualified expenditures from 25 percent to 40 percent, or 50 percent for eligible small businesses. The maximum credit amount is raised to $500,000, or $600,000 for eligible small businesses, with amounts indexed for inflation after 2026. An "eligible small business" is defined using a five-year gross receipts test, and the credit now applies to services provided through third-party intermediaries or jointly owned facilities. These changes apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70402**
The adoption credit is enhanced by making the first $5,000 of the credit refundable, allowing taxpayers to receive a refund even if they have no tax liability. All dollar limits within the adoption credit, including the refundable portion, are adjusted for inflation starting with taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024. The refundable portion is excluded from the carryforward rules for unused credits. These amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70403**
This section amends the adoption credit rules to recognize Indian tribal governments alongside state governments when determining if a child has special needs. This recognition allows children adopted through tribal government determinations to qualify for the enhanced adoption credit. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70404**
The exclusion limit for employer-provided dependent care assistance programs is increased from $5,000 to $7,500 for single filers and from $2,500 to $3,750 for married individuals filing separately. This increase allows employees to exclude a larger amount of employer-provided dependent care benefits from their gross income. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70405**
The child and dependent care tax credit is enhanced by setting the applicable percentage at 50 percent for taxpayers with adjusted gross income (AGI) up to $15,000. The percentage phases down by 1 percentage point for each $2,000 of AGI above $15,000 until it reaches 35 percent, and then phases down further to a floor of 20 percent for AGI exceeding $75,000 ($150,000 for joint returns). This structure provides a higher credit rate for lower-income families compared to previous rules. The changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70411**
This section creates a new tax credit for individuals who make qualified contributions to scholarship granting organizations in participating states, capped at $1,700 per taxpayer. Eligible students must have household incomes not exceeding 300 percent of the area median gross income, and scholarships must fund qualified elementary or secondary education expenses. States must voluntarily elect to participate and certify a list of qualifying scholarship granting organizations, which must spend at least 90 percent of income on scholarships and avoid self-dealing. Additionally, scholarships provided by these organizations for qualified expenses are excluded from the recipient's gross income.

**Sec. 70412**
The exclusion for employer payments of student loans under Section 127 is made permanent by removing the sunset date of January 1, 2026. The annual exclusion limit of $5,250 is now subject to inflation adjustments for taxable years beginning after 2026. These changes apply to payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70413**
Qualified higher education expenses for 529 accounts are expanded to include tuition, curriculum materials, books, online materials, tutoring by qualified instructors, testing fees, dual enrollment fees, and educational therapies for students with disabilities at elementary and secondary schools. The annual limit for tax-free distributions for K-12 tuition is increased from $10,000 to $20,000. The expansion of expense types applies to distributions made after the date of enactment, while the increased limit applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70414**
529 account funds can now be used for "qualified postsecondary credentialing expenses," including tuition, fees, books, and testing costs for recognized credential programs. Recognized programs include those listed on state workforce lists, the Veterans Benefits Administration directory, or those leading to industry-recognized credentials, apprenticeships, or professional licenses. This allows tax-free withdrawals for vocational and professional certification training. The amendments apply to distributions made after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70415**
The excise tax on net investment income of private colleges and universities is modified to use a tiered rate structure based on the institution's student-adjusted endowment: 1.4 percent for endowments between $500,000 and $750,000 per student, 4 percent for $750,000 to $2,000,000, and 8 percent for amounts exceeding $2,000,000. The tax applies to institutions with at least 3,000 tuition-paying students, more than 50 percent of whom are in the U.S., and excludes state colleges. Net investment income calculations now explicitly include interest from student loans and federally-subsidized royalty income. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70416**
The definition of "covered employee" for the excise tax on excess compensation in tax-exempt organizations is expanded to include any current or former employee who worked for the organization during any taxable year beginning after December 31, 2016. This change prevents organizations from avoiding the tax by rotating high-paid employees or excluding former employees from the calculation. The amendment applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70421**
Opportunity Zones are permanently renewed with decennial designations starting July 1, 2026, and every ten years thereafter, replacing the previous sunset provisions. The definition of low-income communities is updated to include tracts with a 20 percent poverty rate and median family income up to 125 percent of the area median, and the special rule for contiguous tracts is repealed. Deferral of capital gains is limited to five years, with a 10 percent basis step-up for investments held five years (30 percent for rural funds), and a 100 percent exclusion for gains on investments held at least ten years. New information reporting requirements are imposed on qualified opportunity funds and businesses, with penalties for non-compliance, and the IRS is appropriated $15 million for annual reporting on the program's impact.

**Sec. 70422**
The permanent state housing credit ceiling for the Low-Income Housing Tax Credit is increased to 1.12 percent of the state population for calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025. The tax-exempt bond financing requirement is relaxed, allowing buildings to qualify for the credit if 50 percent of their basis is financed by tax-exempt bonds, or if 25 percent is financed by bonds issued after December 31, 2025, provided those bonds cover at least 5 percent of the basis. These changes apply to buildings placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70423**
The New Markets Tax Credit is permanently extended for calendar years after 2019, removing the previous expiration date. Unused annual allocation limitations can be carried forward for up to five years, with excess amounts from years before 2026 treated as occurring in 2025. These amendments apply to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70424**
The partial deduction for charitable contributions by individuals who do not itemize is made permanent and increased to $1,000 for single filers and $2,000 for married couples filing jointly. This deduction is available for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70425**
A 0.5 percent floor is imposed on individual charitable deductions, meaning contributions are only deductible to the extent they exceed 0.5 percent of the taxpayer's contribution base. The limitation for cash contributions to public charities is adjusted to allow deductions up to 60 percent of the contribution base, coordinated with other limits. Contributions disallowed by the 0.5 percent floor can be carried forward only if the taxpayer exceeds the overall percentage limitations in the carryover year. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70426**
A 1 percent floor is imposed on corporate charitable deductions, allowing deductions only for contributions exceeding 1 percent of the corporation's taxable income, up to a maximum of 10 percent. Unused contributions can be carried forward for five years, with specific rules for net operating loss carryovers. These amendments apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70427**
The limitation on the cover over of federal excise tax on distilled spirits to Puerto Rico is permanently increased to $13.25 per proof gallon. This change applies to distilled spirits brought into the United States after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70428**
Activities related to participation or investment in fisheries in the Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands by entities identified in the Magnuson-Stevens Act are deemed substantially related to their tax-exempt purposes. Transfers of assets from wholly owned subsidiaries to these entities within 18 months of enactment are treated as non-taxable, and income from these activities is exempt from taxation. This provision remains effective during the existence of the western Alaska community development quota program.

**Sec. 70429**
The maximum charitable deduction for expenses incurred in support of Native Alaskan subsistence whaling is increased from $10,000 to $50,000. This change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70430**
The exception to the percentage-of-completion method of accounting is expanded from "home construction contracts" to "residential construction contracts," with a three-year completion period for non-home residential contracts. This exception also applies for alternative minimum tax purposes. The changes apply to contracts entered into in taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70431**
The exclusion for gain on qualified small business stock (QSBS) is expanded to allow a 100 percent exclusion for stock held at least five years, with phased exclusions of 50 percent for three years and 75 percent for four years. The per-issuer gain exclusion limit is increased to $15 million for stock acquired after enactment (indexed for inflation), up from $10 million, and the aggregate gross assets limit for qualifying corporations is raised to $75 million. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70432**
The de minimis reporting threshold for third-party network transactions is restored to the pre-2021 levels, requiring reporting only if payments exceed $20,000 and there are more than 200 transactions. This repeal of the American Rescue Plan Act's lower thresholds also applies to backup withholding rules. The reinstatement is effective as if included in the American Rescue Plan Act, with backup withholding changes applying to calendar years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70433**
The threshold for information reporting of miscellaneous income (Form 1099-MISC) is increased from $600 to $2,000, with annual inflation adjustments after 2026. This change applies to payments for services and backup withholding rules. The amendments apply to payments made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70434**
Qualified sound recording productions are added to the list of productions eligible for immediate expensing of costs under Section 181, with a dollar limit of $150,000 per production. These productions are also eligible for bonus depreciation. The changes apply to productions commencing in taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70435**
25 percent of the interest received by qualified lenders on loans secured by rural or agricultural real estate is excluded from gross income. Qualified lenders include banks, insurance companies, and certain farm credit entities, and qualified property includes land used for farming, fishing, or aquaculture. This exclusion applies to taxable years ending after the date of enactment.

**Sec. 70436**
The federal transfer and manufacturing taxes on firearms are reduced to $0 for all firearms except machineguns and destructive devices, which remain taxed at $200. This effectively eliminates the National Firearms Act tax for most regulated firearms. The changes apply to calendar quarters beginning more than 90 days after enactment.

**Sec. 70437**
Taxpayers selling qualified farmland property to qualified farmers can elect to pay the tax on the gain in four equal annual installments. Qualified farmland must have been used for farming for at least 10 years and be subject to a 10-year restriction on non-farm use. The election must be made by the due date of the tax return for the year of sale.

**Sec. 70438**
The rules for treating certain disaster-related personal casualty losses as fully deductible are extended by substituting the date of enactment of this Act for the previous enactment date in the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2020. This allows taxpayers to deduct losses from disasters occurring after the original act's enactment date without the usual limitations.

**Sec. 70439**
The asset test for taxable REIT subsidiaries is restored by increasing the maximum percentage of a REIT's assets that can be held in taxable REIT subsidiaries from 20 percent to 25 percent. This change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


